! Ironworks

Settings for the Ironworks theme.

!! Layout

<$radio tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/sidebarlayout" value="fluid-fixed"> Fluid story river, fixed sidebar</$radio>

<$radio tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/sidebarlayout" value="fixed-fluid"> Fixed story river, fluid sidebar</$radio>

> In ''fixed-fluid'' the story river keeps its width, so hiding the sidebar is composited entirely on the GPU — nothing reflows. In ''fluid-fixed'' the river reclaims the freed space, which is a layout change by definition; it is interpolated rather than snapped, so it glides, but it is not GPU work.

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/fullwidth" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="no"> Full width — story river and tiddlers take all the space the sidebar is not using</$checkbox>

> Full width lifts the cap on tiddler width and the 68-character measure on tiddler text, so tiddlers fill the story river. The sidebar keeps the sizing its layout mode gives it. In ''fluid-fixed'' the river takes the viewport minus the fixed sidebar. In ''fixed-fluid'' the sidebar is fluid and owns the space beside the river while it is visible, so the river only expands to the full viewport once the sidebar is hidden. On a wide display full width means lines longer than is comfortable to read, which is why it is off by default.

!! Surface

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/patina" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="yes"> Let the plates wear a patina — craquelure, dust and rust</$checkbox>

> The patina is painted under the text, never over it, and it is pitched at the edge of visibility: the test is not whether you can see it but whether you can read a paragraph without noticing it. Switching it off wire-brushes the plates back to bare metal.

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/screws" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="yes"> Fasten each plate down with a screw in every corner</$checkbox>

> Drawn in CSS — three gradients per screw, lit from the same direction as every other edge in the theme. Every slot sits at its own angle: CSS has no `random()`, so the angles are dealt out by arithmetic on each tiddler's position in the river, which means no two screws in a plate agree and no two plates in a river repeat — and a tiddler's screws keep their angles when it re-renders, which a real random would not.

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/scribededitor" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="yes"> Rule the editor like drafting stock</$checkbox>

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/stickytitles" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="no"> Keep tiddler titles visible while scrolling</$checkbox>

!! Sidebar

<$radio tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/sidebarside" value="end"> Sidebar on the trailing edge</$radio>

<$radio tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/sidebarside" value="start"> Sidebar on the leading edge</$radio>

> Leading and trailing rather than left and right, because the layout is written with logical properties: in a left-to-right wiki the trailing edge is the right, in a right-to-left wiki it is the left. The slide direction follows, so the sidebar always leaves toward the edge it is anchored to.

!! Controls

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/toolbaralways" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="no"> Keep tiddler toolbars visible instead of revealing them on hover</$checkbox>

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/toolbarhover" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="yes"> Draw a background behind toolbar buttons on hover</$checkbox>

<$checkbox tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/options/pressanimation" field="text" checked="yes" unchecked="no" default="yes"> Let keys and tags travel when pressed</$checkbox>

> The press movement is a single pixel downwards on `:active` and nothing else. It is switched off automatically for anyone whose system asks for reduced motion; this setting is for turning it off without that.

