|<$link to="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/sidebarbreakpoint"><<lingo Metrics/SidebarBreakpoint>></$link><br>//<<lingo Metrics/SidebarBreakpoint/Hint>>// |^<$edit-text tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/sidebarbreakpoint" default="" tag="input" inputActions=<<sync-sidebarbreakpoint>>/> |
|<$link to="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/sidebarwidth"><<lingo Metrics/SidebarWidth>></$link><br>//<<lingo Metrics/SidebarWidth/Hint>>// |^<$edit-text tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/sidebarwidth" default="" tag="input"/> |
|<$link to="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/storygutter">Story gutter</$link><br>//the space between the story river and the sidebar// |^<$edit-text tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/storygutter" default="" tag="input"/> |
|<$link to="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/platemargin">Plate margin</$link><br>//the width of the plate margin, the wider gutter down the leading edge of a tiddler// |^<$edit-text tiddler="$:/themes/BurningTreeC/vanilla-ironworks/metrics/platemargin" default="" tag="input"/> |

> ''Sidebar breakpoint'' is written through to <$link to="$:/themes/tiddlywiki/vanilla/metrics/sidebarbreakpoint">Vanilla's own metric</$link> as you type it. Plugins read that tiddler directly to place their own responsive rules - the menubar builds a media query from it, and there is no way for this theme to reach inside a media query and correct the number afterwards - so the two have to agree or a plugin will switch layout at a different width than the theme does.
>
> ''Story left'' and ''story right'' keep Vanilla's metric names so that settings carry across, but this theme lays out with logical properties. They measure from the ''leading'' edge — the left in a left-to-right wiki, the right in a right-to-left one — which is why the hints above are worded differently from Vanilla's.
>
> ''Story top'' moves the story river and only the story river. The sidebar is pinned to the viewport, so it keeps its own top edge; the only thing that pushes it down is a menubar, which has to be cleared or the sidebar would scroll underneath it. Vanilla applies this metric to both, which means you cannot indent the river from the top of the page there without the sidebar dropping with it.
>
> It is also a starting offset rather than the final one: when the menubar plugin is installed, its measured height is added to whatever you set here, so the river clears the bar without you having to work the number out by hand.

